{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>To him to whom it belongs, he shall give it.</b> To him whose property it is, that is, to the one robbed, for he is obligated to restore the stolen object to the victim himself. Or the sense of לאשר הוא לו is: to him who is fit for the property to be his, meaning he restores it to the heirs of the victim if the victim has died." "<b>On the day of his guilt.</b> On the day he reflects and takes to heart his guilt that he robbed him — this is ביום אשמתו — and it also includes the day he robbed him. This teaches you that he stands in his rebellion all the while he does not return it, for transgresses are of two kinds: the first, transgressions between a person and the Place (i.e., HaShem), in which the soul becomes estranged from the Holy One, blessed be He, through evil thoughts and evil deeds, and the sinner in this does violence only to his own soul, as it is written (Proverbs 6): ״משחית נפשו הוא יעשנה״; and the second, transgressions between a person and his fellow, such as one who disparages him in speech or strikes him on his body — this sinner sins against one person alone. But robbery is an all-inclusive sin, for he sins also against Elohim; therefore the prohibition of robbery is severe, in the manner that our Rabbis of blessed memory said: a seah full of transgressions — none prosecutes for all of them except robbery. And the generation of the Flood — their decree was not sealed except on account of robbery, as it is said (Genesis 6): ״קץ כל בשר וגו׳ כי מלאה הארץ חמס מפניהם״. And so we find in Ezekiel the prophet, peace be upon him, that he enumerated for Israel several transgressions in the passage (Ezekiel 22): ״התשפוט את עיר הדמים והודעתה את וגו׳״, as it is written: ״בדמך אשר שפכת אשמת ובגלוליך אשר עשית טמאת וגו׳״, and he said: ״קדשי בזית ואת שבתותי חללת״, and he said: ״אנשי רכיל היו בך למען שפך דם נשך ותרבית לקחת וגו׳ ואותי שכחת נאם ה׳״, and last he mentioned robbery, as it is said: ״והנה הכיתי כפי אל בצעך אשר עשית״." "<b>And the way</b> of the parable — He did not clap His hands until He mentioned robbery, because of the severity of the prohibition contained in it; and after it He said (Ezekiel 22:14): ״היעמוד לבך אם תחזקנה ידיך לימים אשר אני עושה אותך״. And even though the transgression of robbery is a negative commandment that is severed by a positive commandment and has a remedy through restoration, nonetheless the prohibition should be very severe, for sometimes the victim cannot be found, or he has died, or he dwells in a distant place; or if the victim is found, he may not wish to forgive him. Also, sometimes his property is lost and he lacks the means to restore the stolen object, for wealth is prone to exchange — it shifts from place to place; if today it is near to wealth, in the blink of an eye it can be farther from him than the height of the eagle's flight. And so Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Proverbs 23): ״התעיף עיניך בו ואיננו כי עשה יעשה לו כנפים כנשר יעוף השמים״. Also, sometimes the robber cannot identify the victim, such as one who robs from many — to whom shall he return, when he does not know whom he robbed? And concerning this the verse says: ״ואת אשמו יביא לה׳״ — for after he has restored the stolen object and cleansed himself of it, the verse required of him an offering, because of the severity of the prohibition. For as long as the stolen object remains in his possession, the offering is of no avail and repentance does not atone, since repentance does not emerge from words alone but from deed, and the deed is the restoration of the stolen object. This is what the prophet said (Isaiah 1): ״רחצו הזכו הסירו רוע מעלליכם וגו׳״, and he said (Isaiah 1): ״למדו היטב״, and it is written (Isaiah 1): ״אם יהיו חטאיכם כשנים כשלג ילבינו״. And Job's companions said to him (Job 11): ״אם און בידך הרחיקהו ואל תשכן באהליך עולה״ — they warn in this verse concerning the matter of robbery, both in the open and in the hidden: ״אם און בידך הרחיקהו״ — this is in the open; ״ואל תשכן באהליך עולה״ — this is in the hidden, meaning, that which is hidden inside his tent he must search out and remove from there if it is robbery. And this is what he said afterward (Job 11): ״כי אז תשא פניך ממום והיית מוצק ולא תירא״ — ״כי אז תשא פניך ממום״, meaning, you will be able to walk with an uncovered face regarding that which is in the open; ״והיית מוצק ולא תירא״, meaning, you will be firm in the secret and have nothing to fear in the hidden. From this King David, peace be upon him, said, speaking in prayer and confession (Psalms 51): ״לך לבדך חטאתי״ — this teaches that he was clean of the sin of robbery and had no sin against any person, but only between himself and the Place, may He be exalted; and this is what he said: ״לך לבדך חטאתי״. And so we find that he prayed to HaShem, may He be blessed, in another place, that He cleanse him from hidden things — and these are the transgressions he committed unwittingly and does not remember — as he said (Psalms 19): ״שגיאות מי יבין מנסתרות נקני״, for from intentional sins he guarded himself and was careful not to stumble in them. And it is well known that all who guard themselves from them — their reward is with them and their deed is before them, and they merit the great hidden good, as he said (Psalms 19): ״גם עבדך נזהר בהם בשמרם עקב רב״."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {2 "Opening phrase <b>ודרך</b> משל appears truncated or elliptical in the source; rendered hyper-literally without smoothing. The flow seems to continue directly from the preceding comment's discussion of Ezekiel 22."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:23:58.779893255Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}